611:
A. All general assistance requests shall be investigated within a 24-hour period. In no instance should an application involving an emergency be delayed until the next day.
B. The general assistance program provides assistance to persons who are unemployable, and who meet certain eligibility requirements. In order to receive general assistance a person must:
1. Be unemployable;
2. Be in need according to standards hereinafter prescribed;
3. Not be receiving any type of monetary assistance except in extreme situations approved by the DFACS board;
4. Meet residence requirements.
(Res. of 6-13-83, § 2(1); Code 1991, § 8.08.070)

613:
For purposes of this chapter, the following criteria are to be used to determine need:
A. A recipient and spouse may both be eligible;
B. A child under 16 years of age may be included in the determination of need until the family is certified for AFDC;
C. A child over 16 years of age who is mentally or physically unable to work may be included in determination of need;
D. An applicant/recipient will not be ineligible by virtue of having a relative living with him if that relative would meet all eligibility requirements;
E. All resources available to the applicant/recipient shall be taken into consideration, [including the following,] but not limited thereto:
1. Allowable reserve. A maximum of $200.00 allowable for general assistance recipients;
2. Real and personal property. No person who claims any taxable real estate shall be eligible except in cases of extreme hardship. The cases of extreme hardship shall be determined by the sole discretion of the DFACS board. Ownership of tools, machinery, an automobile, or any such personal property will be allowed only when essential to the work of the applicant/recipient upon his recovery from incapacity;
3. Income. If an applicant/recipient has had any income during the two weeks prior to his application, he is ineligible. General assistance is never to be utilized to supplement income except in extreme cases which are approved by the DFACS board;
4. Responsible relatives. An applicant/recipient is ineligible for general assistance if he has responsible relatives capable of providing assistance.
(Res. of 6-13-83, § 2(2)(B); Code 1991, § 8.08.090)

662:
The sanitary landfill shall be operated in a safe and sanitary manner, and shall conform with all appropriate state and federal laws and regulations. Included among these regulations and requirements are the following:
A. All solid waste shall be compacted and covered with a minimum of six inches of clean soil or state approved alternative daily cover at the end of each day of operation;
B. No burning of waste is permitted;
C. When each portion of the sanitary landfill reaches its final grade, two feet of final cover will be placed on top of the lift, graded to drain and minimize erosion, and seeded to establish a stable vegetative cover.
(Res. of 5-26-94; Code 1991, § 8.24.240)

667:
A. None of the following shall be deposited or dumped at the sanitary landfill or at any county solid waste compactor site:
1. Waste originating outside of Hall County;
2. Liquid waste, except used motor oil for recovery;
3. Regulated quantities of hazardous waste as defined in the rules promulgated by the board of natural resources, chapter 391-3-11;
4. Septic tank pumpings and sewage;
5. Tires;
6. Yard trimmings;
7. Sludges which, upon certified laboratory testing, meet the definition of hazardous waste as defined herein. Industrial and commercial generators wishing to dispose of sludges in Hall County solid waste handling facilities shall first furnish test results, due annually January 1 of each year, certifying that such sludges are deemed non-hazardous;
8. Boats of any size, including kayaks, canoes, flat bottom boats, and motorized boats;
9. Bulk styrofoam, including styrofoam used for floatation;
10. Pressurized tanks of any type, including air tanks, gasoline tanks, and propane tanks; and
11. Mobile homes.
The county's board of commissioners may, at their election, allow non-profit corporations or other charitable entities to deposit some or all of the above-referenced materials in exceptions for special events that serve community or civic purposes.
B. It shall be unlawful to place, abandon, leave, keep or store junk and salvage material, including non-functioning motor vehicles, appliances, machinery, equipment or parts thereof, out of doors upon any public or private land, except within the confines of a permitted junk or salvage yard or designated public disposal area. All such materials shall be stored in such manner that will preclude the harborage of rodents and insects.
(Res. of 5-26-94; Code 1991, § 8.24.290; Res. of 7-14-22(1), § 1; Res. of 4-9-26(1), §§ 1,2)
